A database release becomes interesting when a feature maps to a query or schema decision you already have. PostgreSQL 18 offers several such changes. Three I would put on a small evaluation list are B-tree skip scans, virtual generated columns, and built-in UUIDv7 generation.

The [release announcement](https://www.postgresql.org/about/news/postgresql-18-released-3142/) describes a much wider set of improvements. This article focuses on a few application-level decisions; it makes no claim that an upgrade will automatically speed up a particular workload.

## An index can help beyond its first column

Suppose a sample orders table has an index on state and creation time:

```sql
CREATE INDEX orders_state_created_idx
ON orders (state, created_at);

EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
SELECT id, created_at
FROM orders
WHERE created_at >= TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-09-01 00:00:00+00';
```

This is an illustrative query for a test database containing those columns. `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` executes the query, so evaluate expensive statements on representative staging data first.

The query omits the index's leading state column. PostgreSQL 18's skip-scan optimisation can make repeated index searches useful when the planner expects relatively few distinct leading values. With many distinct values, another plan may be cheaper. [The multicolumn-index documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html) explains those conditions.

I would compare plans on the same dataset before removing any existing index. Check estimated versus actual rows, buffers accessed, and timing across repeated runs. Data distribution matters: a tiny demonstration table tells little about a production reporting query.

## Choose when a derived value is calculated

PostgreSQL 18 supports virtual generated columns, whose values are computed when read. Stored generated columns keep the computed value in storage. The [generated-column documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-generated-columns.html) also specifies restrictions on allowed expressions.

For a simple test schema:

```sql
CREATE TABLE example_order_lines (
  quantity integer NOT NULL CHECK (quantity > 0),
  unit_price numeric(12, 2) NOT NULL CHECK (unit_price >= 0),
  line_total numeric GENERATED ALWAYS AS
    (quantity * unit_price) VIRTUAL
);
```

The choice I would examine is repeated calculation versus storage and write-time maintenance. A cheap expression read occasionally is a different case from a costly value used in every report.

For financial records, there is another design question: should a field represent the current calculation or a historical amount agreed at purchase? That is a business rule to settle before choosing a generated column. This example demonstrates syntax, not a complete invoicing model.

## UUIDv7 deserves a measured comparison

PostgreSQL 18 provides `uuidv7()` alongside existing UUID functions. Version 7 incorporates a time component, which gives it a different ordering profile from randomly distributed identifiers. See the [UUID reference](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-uuid.html) for the function's behaviour.

I would assess identifier changes using insert throughput, index growth, and the application's existing contracts. Replacing IDs in a running system involves references, integrations, and migrations. It should not begin with changing a default expression and hoping downstream consumers adapt.

My first experiment would be a separate test table populated with representative data. A useful result includes both the measurements and the migration cost. That makes it possible to decide whether a new feature actually earns its place in the application.
